{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>כי וגו׳.</b> Some say סאון is from the root of סאה, that HaShem measured out to Assyria according to its deeds, and some say it is the reverse of אסון, like כשב and כבש. The correct view is that this word has no parallel, and the proof is סואן, whose vav is a root letter and the nun is a root letter, and its meaning is like נאקה, with a great noise:" "<b>ושמלה מגוללה בדמים.</b> Each one lies slain and his garment is rolled in his blood; or its meaning is the garment that was rolled in the blood of the slain he killed, and so it was for burning, and the sense concerns the death of the host of Sennacherib at the hand of the angel, like those burned <small>(Isaiah 37:36)</small>:"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra argues the vav and nun are both root letters of סאון/סואה; the grammatical reasoning is compressed and follows the medieval debate over whether this is a triliteral root.", 1 "Resolved 'חיל סנחריב' as 'the host of Sennacherib' since חיל here means army/troops; 'חלל' rendered singular to match Ibn Ezra's singular phrasing 'כל אחד שוכב חלל.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:53:01.674432694Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}